-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 20261117~sparky9~0 (2026-11-17)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * A screen that arrives after the splash was laid out gets the splash laid out again (ORDER 639; Daniel, 2026-11-17:
    "it's displaying a flat sbs image"; "if it's saved mode is 2D, then it should display only one eye"). The theme sized
    and placed its pictures once, for the screens there when it loaded: on a machine whose GPU driver comes after
    Plymouth's device timeout (the A10: the firmware's framebuffer at 1024x768, amdgpu about 17 s in) the eyes stayed laid
    out for the framebuffer, and a television connected later showed the left picture with the right one beside it.
    layout () now sizes and places the background, the swirl, the logo and the electrons' orbits for the window as it is,
    at load and at each display hotplug (Plymouth.SetDisplayHotplugFunction); the sprites are made once and keep their
    places in the drawing order. The depth limits are read again with the window
  * A hotplug that leaves no display (the firmware's framebuffer gone before the GPU's screens come) keeps the layout
    as it was: with no display the window has no size and no depth limits, and the next display lays it out (found in
    the rig: without this the script stopped at every frame of that gap, "Cannot negate non number objects")
  * Needs plymouth 26.134.222-1+stereo3d4, whose script plugin keeps the window in both eyes for every screen of a stereo
    boot (a screen that cannot show them shows the left eye) and sizes the eyes for the main screen

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 20261109~sparky9~0 (2026-11-09)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * The splash follows the person's depth limits (ORDER 481; Daniel, 2026-11-05: "read the depth config by, and in this
    order of importance: 1 - User choice (but based on 2); 2 - Current display"). It had levels fixed at the desktop's
    default limits, so a person who lowered PoppedLimit still saw the default depth at boot. The theme now asks Plymouth for
    the main screen's limits (Window.GetStereoPoppedLimit and GetStereoSunkLimit, plymouth stereo3d-0003; the boot passes
    them in plymouth.stereo= as popped= and sunk=): the base is half the popped limit, the logo and the nearest electron the
    popped limit, the farthest electron 0.6 of the sunk limit from the base. At the defaults, 4 and 10, the levels are the
    ones of the previous version (2, 4 and 4 px, the farthest 4 behind), so nothing is raised
  * Needs plymouth 26.134.222-1+stereo3d3
  * tests/depth-limits.py takes the limits as arguments and checks the placement against them at five eye widths

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 20261108~sparky9~0 (2026-11-08)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * The splash stays inside the desktop's own depth limits (ORDER 472; Daniel, 2026-11-08: "on plymouth - the separation is
    huge", on a laptop panel in red/cyan). Scaling by the eye's width was right (the same rule as Stereo KWin's), but the
    levels were fixed above the desktop's popped limit: the logo 9.08 px, the base 4.54 and a popping electron 8.54 at 1920,
    against 4 px (kwinrc [StereoDepth] PoppedLimit); at 1366 per eye 5 px against 3. Now the base is half the popped
    limit, the logo and the nearest electron the popped limit (2, 4 and 4 px at 1920), and the farthest electron sinks 4,
    inside the sunk limit of 10
  * A level is placed by its whole disparity, rounded to a pixel with the odd pixel to the left eye; each eye rounded its
    half before, which made every disparity even and, at a few hundred pixels per eye, put base and logo on one level
  * tests/depth-limits.py checks the generated script against the desktop's limits at 1280, 1366, 1920, 2560 and 3840

